  "id": "DrugBank_DB00041 - MONDO_15667",
  "verbalization": "Aldesleukin, a cytokine with a well-documented role in immune modulation, is indicated for Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) cases with the specific chromosomal translocation T(6;9)(p23;q34). This translocation is associated with a subset of AMLs that are often resistant to conventional therapies. The evidence suggests that Aldesleukin may be repurposed for this subset of AMLs through its interaction with the disease pathway, as it is indicated in conjunction with other drugs like Gilteritinib, Ivosidenib, and Decitabine, which are used in the treatment of AML. These drugs target various molecular pathways, including those involved in leukemia progression and resistance. The shared indication for AML with this translocation across multiple drugs implies a potential mechanistic link, possibly involving immune modulation or anti-leukemic effects that are relevant to this subset of patients. The high scores in the evidence suggest a strong, biologically plausible relationship, supported by the overlapping indications and the presence of a common disease pathway.",
